%N A400282 Number of palindromic diagrams with n cells.
%C A400282 A palindromic diagram of n is an edge-connected, self-conjugate skew Young/Ferrers diagram with n cells, if the string formed by concatenating the rows is a palindromic word over a two-symbol alphabet. We suggest calling these objects 'paligrams', and we will use this newly coined name here.
%C A400282 For example, the 10-cell palindromic diagram (where '*' marks a cell, and 'o' an empty position)
%C A400282   o o * *
%C A400282   o * * *
%C A400282   * * * o
%C A400282   * * o o
%C A400282   forms the palindromic string 'oo**o******o**oo' when read by rows. Reversing this string reverses both the row order and the order within each row. To reconstruct the diagram from string s, split s into consecutive blocks of sqrt(length(s)) symbols, with each block representing a row.
%C A400282 Self-conjugacy means the diagram is invariant under reflection in the main diagonal (transposition), and palindromicity means it is invariant under the half-turn (rotation by 180 degrees). Together, these force a third symmetry, the reflection in the antidiagonal. Hence a paligram is invariant under the full Klein four-group (Vierergruppe) generated by transposition and the half-turn, acting on its bounding box. The bounding box, i.e., the smallest rectangle containing the diagram, is a square. Thus a paligram is symmetric with respect to both diagonals of this square and under the half-turn about its center.
%C A400282 Each row is a single contiguous nonempty run of '*' cells (this follows from the skew shape and the edge-connectivity), and by symmetry the same holds for each column. The top row touches the right edge, and the bottom row touches the left edge. As one moves downward, both ends of the run move weakly left, and consecutive runs share a column.
%C A400282 The paligram-Dyck path bijection: Let G be a paligram with n cells and square width h. Read the numbers of occupied cells on successive diagonals parallel to the main diagonal, from bottom-left to top-right. Interpret these numbers as the heights P_{1}, ..., P_{2*h-1} of a lattice path, with initial and final heights P_0 = P_{2*h} = 0. The resulting path is a symmetric primitive Dyck path of semilength h and area n. Here the area of a Dyck path P of semilength h is defined as the sum of its intermediate heights, area(P) = Sum_{j=1..2*h-1} P_i. Under the bijection, this equals the number of occupied cells of the corresponding paligram.
%C A400282 Conversely, every symmetric primitive Dyck path determines a paligram by placing, on each corresponding diagonal of an h X h square, a centered consecutive block of cells whose length equals the path height. The two constructions are inverse to each other. In particular, the bijection preserves the number of cells as area and square width as semilength.
%C A400282 For each n >= 19 at least one paligram with n cells exists. (No paligrams exist for n in {0, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 11, 12, 18}.)
%D A400282 Richard P. Stanley, Enumerative Combinatorics, Vol. 2, 2nd ed. (2023), see especially Chapter 7.
%H A400282 Peter Luschny, <a href="/A400282/b400282.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A400282 M. P. Delest and J. M. Fedou, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0012-365X(93)90224-H">Enumeration of skew Ferrers diagrams</a>, Discrete Mathematics, vol. 112 (1993), no. 1-3, pp. 65-79.
%H A400282 DENG Li-hua, DENG Yu-ping and Louis W. Shapiro, <a href="https://doi.org/10.6040/j.issn.1671-9352.0.2014.196">The Riordan group and symmetric lattice paths</a>, Journal of Shandong University, 2015, 50(04): 82-89. (in Chinese).
%H A400282 Sergi Elizalde, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2021.112364">Symmetric peaks and symmetric valleys in Dyck paths</a>, Discrete Mathematics, Volume 344, Issue 6, 2021.
%H A400282 Rigoberto Flórez and José L. Ramírez, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2020.112118">Enumerating symmetric and asymmetric peaks in Dyck paths</a>, Discrete Mathematics, Volume 343, Issue 12, 2020.
%H A400282 Nils Haug and Thomas Prellberg, <a href="https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1412.5108">Uniform asymptotics of area-weighted Dyck paths</a>, Journal of Mathematical Physics, vol. 56:4 (2015); also arXiv:1412.5108 [math-ph].
%H A400282 Pierre Leroux and Etienne Rassart, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9901135">Enumeration of Symmetry Classes of Parallelogram Polyominoes</a>, arXiv:math/9901135 [math.CO], 1999. (p. 3, formula 8).
%H A400282 Peter Luschny, <a href="/A400282/a400282.png">Paligram and Dyck Path</a>, an illustration.
%H A400282 Peter Luschny, <a href="https://github.com/PeterLuschny/Gists/blob/main/GeneratingPaligrams.ipynb">Generating Paligrams</a>, A SageMath notebook, Oct 2026.
%H A400282 MathOverflow, <a href="https://mathoverflow.net/q/515969">Generating function for symmetric primitive Dyck paths</a>, Q-515969, Oct 13 2026.
%F A400282 Recurrence: a(n) = Sum_{h in H} f(h - 3, floor((h*h - n)/2), h), where H = {1 <= h <= floor((n + 2)/3) and h = n (mod 2)}, and f(k, m, c) = [m = 0] + Sum_{u <= a <= v} f(k - 2, m - 2*a - 1, a - 1), where u = floor(sqrt(max(m - 1, 0))) and v = min(c, k, floor((m - 1)/2)).
%F A400282 O.g.f.: Define Phi(z; q) = Sum_{j>=0} (-1)^j * q^(j*(j-1)) * z^j / (q; q)_j, where (q; q)_j = Product_{i=1..j} (1 - q^i), (q; q)_0 = 1 is the q-Pochhammer function. Define A(x) = (x / Phi(x^6; x^4)) * Sum_{r>=0} x^(r*(r + 2)) * Phi(x^(4*r + 10); x^4). Then a(n) = [x^n] A(x). (In the linked notebook, it is proven that this is also the generating function for symmetric primitive Dyck paths.)
%F A400282 Empirically: a(n) ~ c_{e/o} * b^n, where b = 1.12336016..., c_{e} = 0.18051932... if n is even, and c_{o} = 0.20637554... if n is odd. Apparently b = A401308^(-1), where A401308 is defined as the smallest positive solution of Phi(x^6; x^4) = 0.
%e A400282 All paligrams that exist for n <= 18 are, written as words:
%e A400282    1 '*'
%e A400282    4 '****'
%e A400282    7 'o*******o'
%e A400282    9 '*********'
%e A400282   10 'oo**o******o**oo'
%e A400282   13 'ooo**oo***o***o***oo**ooo'
%e A400282   14 'o**************o'
%e A400282   15 'ooo**o****o***o****o**ooo'
%e A400282   16 '****************'
%e A400282   16 'oooo**ooo***oo***oo***oo***ooo**oooo'
%e A400282   17 'oo***oo***********oo***oo'
%p A400282 f := proc(k, m, c) option remember; local a, u, o;
%p A400282 u := floor(sqrt(max(m - 1, 0))); o := min(c, k, iquo(m - 1, 2));
%p A400282 add(f(k - 2, m - 2*a - 1, a - 1), a = u..o) + ifelse(m = 0, 1, 0) end:
%p A400282 A400282 := n -> local h; add(f(h - 3, iquo(h*h - n, 2), h),
%p A400282 h = 2 - irem(n, 2) .. iquo(n + 2, 3), 2): seq(A400282(n), n = 0..71);
%t A400282 f[k_Integer, m_Integer, c_Integer] := f[k, m, c] = Module[{aMin, aMax}, aMin = Floor[Sqrt[Max[m - 1, 0]]]; aMax = Min[c, k, Floor[(m - 1)/2]]; Boole[m == 0] + If[aMin <= aMax, Total @ Table[f[k-2, m - 2*a - 1, a-1], {a, aMin, aMax}], 0] ];
%t A400282 A400282[n_Integer] := Total @ Table[f[h - 3, Floor[(h^2 - n)/2], h], {h, 2 - Mod[n, 2], Floor[(n + 2)/3], 2}]; Table[A400282[n], {n, 0, 71}]
%o A400282 (Python)
%o A400282 from functools import cache
%o A400282 from math import isqrt
%o A400282 @cache
%o A400282 def f(k, m, c) -> int:
%o A400282     return (m == 0) + sum(f(k - 2, m - 2 * a - 1, a - 1)
%o A400282     for a in range(isqrt(max(m - 1, 0)), min(c, k, (m - 1) // 2) + 1))
%o A400282 def A400282(n: int) -> int:
%o A400282     return sum(f(h - 3, (h * h - n) // 2, h)
%o A400282     for h in range(2 - n % 2, (n + 2) // 3 + 1, 2))
%o A400282 print([A400282(n) for n in range(72)])
%o A400282 (SageMath) # See the linked notebook.
